{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB00958 - MONDO_16249",
  "verbalization": "Carboplatin, a platinum-based chemotherapy agent, is implicated in the development of Hereditary Site-specific Ovarian Cancer Syndrome through its association with cisplatin, which is a known inducer of ovarian cancer. The evidence suggests that Carboplatin, through its indication for various ovarian tumors such as immature ovarian teratoma, ovarian endometrioid adenocarcinoma, and malignant sex cord stromal tumor of the ovary, ultimately leads to the induction of Hereditary Site-specific Ovarian Cancer Syndrome via cisplatin. The shared pathway of interaction with cisplatin indicates a potential mechanism where Carboplatin may influence the carcinogenic process through the same molecular pathways activated by cisplatin. The consistent scores across the three paths suggest a robust, albeit indirect, relationship, highlighting the role of cisplatin as a critical mediator in this connection. The integration of these pathways underscores the importance of cisplatin in linking Carboplatin to ovarian cancer, emphasizing the need for further investigation into the specific mechanisms by which Carboplatin contributes to this syndrome.",
